            "The foundation of the documentation suite is the Commercial Invoice. Unlike a domestic billing receipt, an international commercial invoice is a statutory customs document sworn under penalty of perjury. It serves as the primary legal evidence establishing the Price Actually Paid or Payable for the merchandise. An international commercial invoice must contain non-negotiable data fields: complete legal names, physical addresses, and tax identification numbers of the seller (exporter) and buyer (importer); detailed commercial description of the merchandise, explicitly avoiding vague terms like 'general merchandise' or 'spare parts'; the 6-digit Harmonized System (HS) code; unit prices, itemized quantities, and total invoice value; the currency of transaction; the governing Incoterms 2020 rule with its exact named geographic place (e.g., 'FCA Shanghai International Airport'); the verified country of origin; and the manufacturer's identification code.",
